# Milestone: M3
# Covers: when all strands are on the sleep queue (none on the ready queue),
#         sched_quiescent returns false (wakeup_pending_count > 0); after the
#         timer fires and strands are moved back to ready, quiescent stays false
#         until they finish, then becomes true.
#
# blocked: post-sleep quiescent transition not observable via urbi_step
# matrix-row: scheduler-quiescent
#
# The `## host: step` / `advance-clock` directives (v0.11.4-cat-f) plus
# sleep() (T43) put a strand on the sleep queue, and `step: <STATE>` already
# exposes the quiescence verdict (QUIESCENT vs RUNNING vs WAKE_AT).  The
# blocker is the second half of this fixture — observing the transition BACK
# to quiescent after the sleeper completes — which depends on the lone-sleeper
# wakeup that urbi_step cannot drive (see wait_sleep_basic.chk for the precise
# mechanism: sched_post_dispatch's sleep-queue wake only runs inside the
# dispatch loop, which a lone sleeper never enters because ready_head is NULL
# while strand_runnable_count stays 1 on the parked loader strand).  So while
# a strand is sleeping, step never advances past RUNNING and never reaches the
# post-completion QUIESCENT state this fixture wants to assert.  Empirically,
# `## host: run sleep(100ms)` then `step` then `advance-clock`/`step` stays at
# step: RUNNING throughout — the QUIESCENT verdict never arrives.
#
# Activating needs the same runtime change called out in wait_sleep_basic.chk
# (pre-dispatch-loop sleep-queue wake / loader runnable-count reconciliation),
# out of scope for test-harness-only T39 work.  The quiescence counter
# semantics are unit-tested directly via the scheduler API.
